terly report, management statement, pro\",\n   \"reference_words\": \"had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"expected\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"reading\": \"July\",\n   \"case\": \"c3/clean-6\",\n   \"attempt\": 3,\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia confirm first customer shipments of its Kyber NVL144 rack architecture for the Vera Rubin Ultra generation by December 31, 2028?\",\n   \"reference_words\": \"had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\",\n   \"kind\": \"expected\"\n  }\n ],\n \"unlisted_parts_new_agreed_cases\": [\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-1\",\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"How much will Nvidia's maximum potential guarantee liability under the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate infrastructure leases be at its next annual report?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The maximum potential amount guaranteed, any amounts paid or accrued, and the remaining term of the guarantees disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\"\n   },\n   \"attempts\": [\n    {\n     \"attempt\": 1,\n     \"question\": \"What maximum potential guarantee liability does Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 annual report (Form 10-K)?\",\n     \"not_judged\": [\n      {\n       \"part\": \"Any amounts paid or accrued under the guarantees\",\n       \"why\": \"This is a separate figure from the maximum potential guarantee liability tracked by the topic's outcome.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"part\": \"The remaining term of the guarantees\",\n       \"why\": \"This is a separate measure, not the maximum potential guarantee liability tracked by the topic's outcome.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"attempt\": 2,\n     \"question\": \"What maximum potential guarantee liability under the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate infrastructure leases will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as reported in the guarantees note?\",\n     \"not_judged\": []\n    },\n    {\n     \"attempt\": 3,\n     \"question\": \"What maximum potential guarantee liability will Nvidia disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as stated in its Form 10-K?\",\n     \"not_judged\": []\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-3\",\n   \"thesis\": {\n    \"description\": \"By how much will contract prices for AI-server memory (HBM/DRAM) — the memory input cost embedded in Nvidia's GPUs — rise or fall in calendar 2027?\",\n    \"outcome\": \"The change in HBM/DRAM contract prices over calendar 2027, and the memory makers' reported average selling prices and memory gross margins for the periods covering 2027.\"\n   },\n   \"attempts\": [\n    {\n     \"attempt\": 1,\n     \"question\": \"What percentage change will TrendForce/DRAMeXchange’s monthly and quarterly releases show in AI-server HBM/DRAM contract prices over calendar 2027?\",\n     \"not_judged\": [\n      {\n       \"part\": \"Memory makers’ reported average selling prices and gross margins for periods covering 2027.\",\n       \"why\": \"These are supporting indicators named in the topic’s outcome, not the contract-price change being tracked.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"attempt\": 2,\n     \"question\": \"By what percentage will HBM/DRAM contract prices for AI-server memory change over calendar 2027, as determined from TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract-price releases?\",\n     \"not_judged\": []\n    },\n    {\n     \"attempt\": 3,\n     \"question\": \"What percentage change in HBM/DRAM contract prices over calendar 2027 will TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract-price releases establish?\",\n     \"not_judged\": [\n      {\n       \"part\": \"Memory makers’ reported average selling prices for periods covering 2027\",\n       \"why\": \"This is a separate reported figure; the tracked outcome is the change in HBM/DRAM contract prices over calendar 2027.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"part\": \"Memory makers’ reported gross margins for periods covering 2027\",\n       \"why\": \"This is a separate reported figure; the tracked outcome is the change in HBM/DRAM contract prices over calendar 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"every_topic_of_the_week_with_what_each_attempt_listed\": [\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-1\",\n   \"description\": \"How much will Nvidia's maximum potential guarantee liability under the SB Energy/OpenAI affiliate infrastructure leases be at its next annual report?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The maximum potential amount guaranteed, any amounts paid or accrued, and the remaining term of the guarantees disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Any amounts paid or accrued under the guarantees\",\n     \"The remaining term of the guarantees\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": []\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-2\",\n   \"description\": \"How much of Nvidia's $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments will remain outstanding or unconditional at its next annual report?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The total supply and capacity purchase commitments and the portion that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"The portion of commitments that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"The portion of the commitments that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"The portion of commitments that is adjustable or cancelable at a cost (the topic’s “unconditional” status).\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-3\",\n   \"description\": \"By how much will contract prices for AI-server memory (HBM/DRAM) — the memory input cost embedded in Nvidia's GPUs — rise or fall in calendar 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The change in HBM/DRAM contract prices over calendar 2027, and the memory makers' reported average selling prices and memory gross margins for the periods covering 2027.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Memory makers’ reported average selling prices and gross margins for periods covering 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"Memory makers’ reported average selling prices for periods covering 2027\",\n     \"Memory makers’ reported gross margins for periods covering 2027\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-4\",\n   \"description\": \"How much combined capital expenditure will the five largest hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — spend in calendar 2026 and calendar 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The big five hyperscalers' combined capex for calendar 2026 and calendar 2027, versus the approximately $800 billion for 2026 and roughly $1.3 trillion for 2027 the article reports they plan to spend.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": \"declined\",\n    \"2\": \"declined\",\n    \"3\": \"declined\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-5\",\n   \"description\": \"Will Nvidia complete its approximately $12.93 billion acquisition of Hugging Face — and at what final cost and regulatory conditions — by the check date?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Closing or termination of the signed Hugging Face acquisition, the final consideration paid (roughly $11.9 billion to shareholders plus up to $1 billion of equity-based retention awards), and any conditions regulators impose.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"The final cost or consideration paid for the acquisition.\",\n     \"The regulatory conditions imposed on the acquisition.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"The final cost or consideration paid for the acquisition.\",\n     \"The regulatory conditions imposed on the acquisition.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"The final consideration paid.\",\n     \"Any conditions regulators impose.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-6\",\n   \"description\": \"Will the U.S. licence requirement on Nvidia's data center chips still bar it from China's data center compute market at Nvidia's fiscal 2027 year-end, or will it be lifted before then?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether the U.S. export-licence requirement on Nvidia's H20-class data center products remains in force or is lifted, and whether Nvidia resumes recognizing data center compute revenue from China.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Whether Nvidia resumes recognizing data center compute revenue from China.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"Whether Nvidia resumes recognizing data-center compute revenue from China.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"Whether Nvidia resumes recognizing data center compute revenue from China.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-7\",\n   \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's Rubin data center GPU — the next generation of the product that carries Nvidia's data center revenue — reach volume ramp in the quarter management describes, or slip into calendar 2027 because of memory shortages?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether Rubin's volume ramp and shipments occur in the quarter Nvidia describes (about 20% of data center revenue) or are pushed into calendar 2027, and whether reported sales timing slips.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Rubin making up about 20% of data center revenue\",\n     \"Memory shortages as the reason a ramp might slip\",\n     \"Whether reported sales timing slips\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"Rubin making up about 20% of data-center revenue\",\n     \"The ramp slipping because of memory shortages\",\n     \"Whether reported sales timing slips\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"Rubin making up about 20% of data-center revenue\",\n     \"The ramp slipping into calendar 2027 because of memory shortages\",\n     \"Whether reported sales timing slips\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-8\",\n   \"description\": \"How much residual-value/loss-share exposure has Nvidia taken on under its customer data center financing support — the commitment to cover up to 25% of the loss if financed chips lose value — and how much is disclosed at its next annual report?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The maximum potential amount of Nvidia's loss coverage on customer data center chip values, any amounts paid or accrued, and the terms and remaining duration of that support, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Any amounts paid or accrued under the support.\",\n     \"The terms and remaining duration of the support.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"Any amounts paid or accrued under the support.\",\n     \"The terms and remaining duration of the support.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"The commitment to cover up to 25% of losses if financed chips lose value.\",\n     \"Any amounts paid or accrued under the support.\",\n     \"The terms and remaining duration of the support.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-9\",\n   \"description\": \"Will Nvidia's Groq 3 LPX rack-scale LPU system — the first product built on the Groq technology it paid about $20 billion to license and staff — reach volume shipments and commercial scale in the quarter Nvidia describes (its fiscal Q3 FY2027), or slip?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether Groq 3 LPX volume shipments occur 'later this quarter' as Nvidia stated, and whether the system begins contributing to reported data center revenue or slips into fiscal Q4 FY2027/calendar 2027.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Whether Groq 3 LPX begins contributing to reported data center revenue or reaches commercial scale.\",\n     \"Whether shipments slip into fiscal Q4 FY2027 or calendar 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"Whether Groq 3 LPX reaches commercial scale or begins contributing to reported data center revenue.\",\n     \"Whether a missed Q3 shipment slips into fiscal Q4 FY2027 or calendar 2027.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"Whether Groq 3 LPX reaches commercial scale.\",\n     \"Whether Groq 3 LPX begins contributing to reported data center revenue.\",\n     \"A slip into fiscal Q4 FY2027/calendar 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-10\",\n   \"description\": \"What will the hourly spot rental price for Nvidia B200 GPUs be at the end of calendar 2026, after more than doubling from March 2026 to October 2026?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The hourly spot rental price for Nvidia B200 GPUs at year-end 2026 and whether it remains above its March 2026 level.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"Whether the price remains above its March 2026 level.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"Whether the year-end 2026 price remains above its March 2026 level.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": \"declined\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-11\",\n   \"description\": \"Will Nvidia sign and complete an acquisition of Reflection AI — and at what consideration and terms — by the check date?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"A definitive agreement to acquire Reflection AI, its closing (or the talks' collapse), and the final consideration paid and any regulatory conditions imposed.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"At what consideration and terms, including any regulatory conditions imposed\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [\n     \"The consideration and other terms of the acquisition, including any regulatory conditions imposed.\"\n    ],\n    \"3\": [\n     \"Sign a definitive agreement to acquire Reflection AI\",\n     \"The consideration and other terms of the acquisition\"\n    ]\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-12\",\n   \"description\": \"How much will global AI capital spending total in calendar 2026 and calendar 2027?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Global AI capex for calendar 2026 and calendar 2027, measured against Goldman Sachs' forecast of $1 trillion of global AI spend this year and AI spend equal to 1.8% of U.S. GDP in 2026, 2.5% in 2027 (and 2.8% in 2028).\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": \"declined\",\n    \"2\": \"declined\",\n    \"3\": \"declined\"\n   }\n  },\n  {\n   \"case\": \"c1/clean-13\",\n   \"description\": \"How much of Nvidia's $25 billion of equity-investment commitments outstanding at July 26, 2026 will be funded or remain outstanding at its next annual report?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"The remaining balance of Nvidia's equity-investment commitments, the cash paid to fund them, and any change in the committed amounts, as disclosed in Nvidia's next annual report.\",\n   \"not_judged_by_attempt\": {\n    \"1\": [\n     \"The cash paid to fund the commitments\",\n     \"Any change in the committed amounts\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": \"declined\",\n    \"3\": \"declined\"\n   }\n  }\n ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-closing.txt clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-schema.json clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md'","aggregated_output":"You are a senior hedge-fund analyst. A research topic was opened on the company from the item below. Write the one question the topic is judged on: what it asks, its answer ladder and its due day. The ladder is written once, from this item only, and is never rewritten. Write in English.\n\nWhat the question asks\n- A judged question concerns one figure for a named period or one event. Write a question, not an assertion. A figure or event must be able to determine the outcome on the due date. For a figure, the question names the period it covers or the day it is measured at, and the material that settles it.\n- A topic's wording may ask about more than one thing. Judge the one the topic's outcome names as what is tracked. List every other part of the topic's wording that the question leaves out, a second figure or event, a condition or a comparison, in not_judged, each with one sentence on why it is not the one tracked. Never fold two things into one ladder: \"Will the regulator approve the drug for a second use, and how many patients will take it in the first year?\" is judged on the approval when that is what its outcome names, and the patient count is listed; \"How much will the carrier's fuel bill be next year, and will its pilots strike?\" is judged on the fuel bill when that is what its outcome names, and the strike is listed.\n- Return no_ladder with the reason, and ladder null, in seven cases: the topic has no main figure or event, naming none, or naming several with none of them the main one; nothing in the topic can be checked on a date, which for an event means that neither the topic nor the item gives a date and the topic names no period, unless it is a pending regulatory decision, for which use the company's current fiscal year-end; the figure is a company's figure for a quarter or a half-year, which is evidence to a yearly question; the figure covers a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve months, and neither the topic nor the item gives the day it is published; the figure is for a year that has already ended on the item's day; the figure is for a year beyond next year, unless the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation; or the question belongs to another company or to a matter that no theme in the request's themes covers, in which case name that company or matter in the reason. Do not rewrite a figure for a year beyond next year as an event to keep it; a numeric completion target may define the state of an event that is itself the topic.\n\nWhose question it is\n- A question about the company's revenue, profit, share, product progress or its own obligations is about the company: object is its ticker, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it. A question about what happens in the world, such as licensing, interest rates or aggregate spending, rather than the resulting effect on one company, belongs to a theme: object is a theme id from the request's themes. A question whose tracked outcome is a government's or regulator's decision to permit, restrict or ban a class of products or trade belongs to the theme that covers it, also when the product is the company's own: whether a health ministry lifts its import ban on one drug maker's vaccine belongs to the theme that covers that country's drug approvals. When the tracked outcome is the company's own sales, shipments or revenue under such a decision, the question is about the company, as is a proceeding against the company itself, such as an investigation of its conduct or a fine. A question about another company's own figure, or about a matter no listed theme covers, has no object here: return no_ladder.\n\nThe ladder\n- Define concrete, verifiable conditions now. Do not write a ladder whose thresholds depend on an unspecified future target. Conditions must not overlap or leave gaps: exactly one must hold when resolved; avoid vague \"partial\" outcomes.\n- An event is answered in event, in at most three outcomes. happened is the condition under which the event the question asks about has happened by the due day. ruled_out says who can rule the event as a whole out before the due day, and how: by_whom names the company, authority, court or party whose decision it would be; decision is one full sentence that names them and says what they decide against the event as a whole, or that they formally abandon or withdraw it, after the item's day. The due day arriving without the event is no one's decision and is never written there: it is what is left. ruled_out is null only when no one's decision can rule the event out before the due day. Write no other condition: what is left, neither of the two, needs none, and where both would hold the event counts as happened. A difference inside an outcome, such as an approval with conditions against one without, is not an outcome of its own; when the topic's wording names it, list it in not_judged. A regulator reviewing a merger gives: happened, the regulator has cleared the merger by the due day; ruled_out, by_whom the regulator or the parties to the merger, decision the regulator blocks the merger or the parties withdraw the filing. An authority's investigation or examination that the question asks to end in a published decision or conclusion always has a ruling out: happened, the authority publishes that decision or conclusion by the due day, whatever it finds; ruled_out, by_whom the authority, decision the authority formally abandons or withdraws the proceeding without it. The proceeding still going on the due day is neither, and a decision to go on with it is not a ruling out. A safety agency examining a carmaker's defect gives: happened, the agency publishes its findings; ruled_out, by_whom the agency, decision the agency formally closes or withdraws the examination without publishing any.\n- A figure is answered in figure: its unit (USD, percent, count, GW, MW, TWD or multiple; other when it is none of these, the question then naming the unit), the one year it covers (written FY2027 for a company's figure, which uses its fiscal year; written 2026 for a theme's figure, which uses the calendar year) and exactly five ranges of that one figure. Each range has an inclusive lower bound lo and an exclusive upper bound hi. Money amounts are written in full (12000000000, not 12 billion); percentages use 73, not 0.73. The lowest range has lo null, the highest hi null; every other bound is a number.\n- Fill exactly one of event and figure; the other is null.\n- reference is what this figure or event is measured against when the topic opens, quoted in the item's own words. Its kind is expected when the item states what the market expects: a consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline. For a figure only, when the item states no expectation and gives the amount most recently reported for the same quantity (the same thing measured the same way: not a part of it, a ceiling on it or another quantity), that amount is the reference and its kind is last_reported; a reported amount is never called an expectation. Otherwise reference is null. value is the number the item states, with value_to its upper end when the item states a range; when the item gives it in words alone, value is null: never supply a number the item does not state. The ranges of a figure state the outcome against a reference that has a value: the middle range holds that value, or runs from at or below a stated range's lower end to at or above its upper end, and the ranges on either side are outcomes above and below it. When reference is null or has no value, the ranges are your own estimate of the outcomes that could happen.\n- favourable_end, for a figure about a company: higher when a higher outcome is good for its business, lower when a lower one is, with one sentence why. A result and its direction are separate: capital spending can build capacity or hurt returns. For an amount the company owes, guarantees or has committed to pay, when neither the topic nor the item settles which end is good for the business, because they argue both ends or say nothing on it, it is lower; when the topic's own words say that more of it is good, it is higher. A retailer's lease guarantees that the item says win store sites and also weigh on its credit are lower; an airline's aircraft purchase commitments that the topic tracks as the fleet growth its plan needs are higher. For a theme's figure it is n/a.\n\nThe due day\n- Set the due day when the question can be resolved. It falls after the item's day, and on or after the day the material that settles the question is published.\n- For a figure, use its publication date, which falls after the end of the period the figure covers: stated when the topic or the item gives it, expected_publication when you set the day you expect it to be published. Company figures use the company's fiscal year: \"this year\" contains the item's publication day, and \"next year\" follows it; convert fiscal years using the fiscal-year note. Other figures use calendar years.\n- For an event, name the date at which its outcome is assessed: stated when the topic or the item gives it; period_end when it is the last day of the period the topic names, or the company's current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision that has no date.\n- When the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation, the due day is at most 548 days (18 months) after the item: for an obligation already taken on, its next annual filing; for one not yet signed, the day by which the signing can be checked.\nBefore you return the answer, check it once. Read the question back: does it ask one thing, the one the topic's outcome names, and is every other part of the topic's wording listed in not_judged? For a figure, check that each range starts exactly where the one below it ends. For an event, check that happened is the event the question asks about; that ruled_out names who decides and a decision of theirs against that event as a whole or its abandonment, never the due day arriving or the event merely being still awaited, and is null only when no one's decision can rule the event out; and that each can be checked on the due day. Check that a reference's kind says truly whether its words state an expectation or the amount already reported for the same quantity, that its value is the number its words state, and, for a figure, that the value falls in the middle range, or that the middle range runs from at or below a stated range's lower end to at or above its upper end, that every bound is a figure and not a future target, and that the due day follows the rules above. For an amount the company owes, guarantees or has committed to pay, check that favourable_end is lower unless the topic or the item settles the other end or the topic's own words say that more of it is good. Correct the answer where a check fails.\n{\n \"type\": \"object\",\n \"additionalProperties\": false,\n \"required\": [\n  \"no_ladder\",\n  \"ladder\"\n ],\n \"properties\": {\n  \"no_ladder\": {\n   \"anyOf\": [\n    {\n     \"type\": \"string\",\n     \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"type\": \"null\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"description\": \"Why no judged question is written, or null: the topic has no main figure or event (none, or several with none the main one); nothing in it can be checked on a date (for an event: no date given and no period named, unless it is a pending regulatory decision, which is due at the company's current fiscal year-end); a company's figure for a quarter or a half-year; a figure for a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve months, with no publication day in the topic or the item; a figure for a year that has already ended on the item's day; a figure for a year beyond next year (unless the topic's opens_as is binding_obligation); or the question belongs to another company or to a matter no listed theme covers (name it). Exactly one of no_ladder and ladder is null (checked by code).\"\n  },\n  \"ladder\": {\n   \"anyOf\": [\n    {\n     \"type\": \"null\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"type\": \"object\",\n     \"additionalProperties\": false,\n     \"required\": [\n      \"question\",\n      \"object\",\n      \"not_judged\",\n      \"event\",\n      \"figure\",\n      \"reference\",\n      \"due_day\",\n      \"due_day_basis\"\n     ],\n     \"properties\": {\n      \"question\": {\n       \"type\": \"string\",\n       \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\",\n       \"description\": \"The one judged question: one figure for a named period, or one event. For a figure it names the period covered or the day measured at, and the material that settles it.\"\n      },\n      \"object\": {\n       \"type\": \"string\",\n       \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\",\n       \"description\": \"The company's ticker, or a theme id from the themes listed in the request. Nothing else: a question with another object is returned as no_ladder.\"\n      },\n      \"not_judged\": {\n       \"type\": \"array\",\n       \"description\": \"Every other part of the topic's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison); empty only when the question leaves nothing of the wording out.\",\n       \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"additionalProperties\": false,\n        \"required\": [\n         \"part\",\n         \"why\"\n        ],\n        \"properties\": {\n         \"part\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\",\n          \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\"\n         },\n         \"why\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\",\n          \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\"\n         }\n        }\n       }\n      },\n      \"event\": {\n       \"description\": \"For an event; else null. Exactly one of event and figure is not null (checked by code).\",\n       \"anyOf\": [\n        {\n         \"type\": \"null\"\n        },\n        {\n         \"type\": \"object\",\n         \"additionalProperties\": false,\n         \"required\": [\n          \"happened\",\n          \"ruled_out\"\n         ],\n         \"properties\": {\n          \"happened\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\",\n           \"description\": \"The condition under which the event the question asks about has happened by the due day.\"\n          },\n          \"ruled_out\": {\n           \"description\": \"Who can rule the event as a whole out before the due day, and how. Null only when no one's decision can. No condition is written for what is left.\",\n           \"anyOf\": [\n            {\n             \"type\": \"null\"\n            },\n            {\n             \"type\": \"object\",\n             \"additionalProperties\": false,\n             \"required\": [\n              \"by_whom\",\n              \"decision\"\n             ],\n             \"properties\": {\n              \"by_whom\": {\n               \"type\": \"string\",\n               \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\",\n               \"description\": \"The company, authority, court or party whose decision it would be.\"\n              },\n              \"decision\": {\n               \"type\": \"string\",\n               \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\",\n               \"description\": \"One full sentence that names them and says what they decide against the event as a whole, or that they formally abandon or withdraw it, after the item's day. Never the due day arriving without the event.\"\n              }\n             }\n            }\n           ]\n          }\n         }\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      \"figure\": {\n       \"description\": \"For one figure: its unit, the year it covers, exactly five ranges and, for a company, the favourable end; else null.\",\n       \"anyOf\": [\n        {\n         \"type\": \"null\"\n        },\n        {\n         \"type\": \"object\",\n         \"additionalProperties\": false,\n         \"required\": [\n          \"unit\",\n          \"year\",\n          \"ranges\",\n          \"favourable_end\",\n          \"favourable_why\"\n         ],\n         \"properties\": {\n          \"unit\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"enum\": [\n            \"USD\",\n            \"percent\",\n            \"count\",\n            \"GW\",\n            \"MW\",\n            \"TWD\",\n            \"multiple\",\n            \"other\"\n           ],\n           \"description\": \"other when the unit is none of the listed; the question then names it.\"\n          },\n          \"year\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"pattern\": \"^(FY)?\\\\d{4}$\",\n           \"description\": \"The one year the figure covers: FY2027 for a company's figure (its fiscal year); 2026 for a theme's figure (a calendar year). Code refuses the other form.\"\n          },\n          \"ranges\": {\n           \"type\": \"array\",\n           \"items\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"additionalProperties\": false,\n            \"required\": [\n             \"lo\",\n             \"hi\"\n            ],\n            \"properties\": {\n             \"lo\": {\n              \"anyOf\": [\n               {\n                \"type\": \"number\"\n               },\n               {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n               }\n              ],\n              \"description\": \"Inclusive lower bound, a number; null for the lowest range only.\"\n             },\n             \"hi\": {\n              \"anyOf\": [\n               {\n                \"type\": \"number\"\n               },\n               {\n                \"type\": \"null\"\n               }\n              ],\n              \"description\": \"Exclusive upper bound, a number; null for the highest range only.\"\n             }\n            }\n           }\n          },\n          \"favourable_end\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"enum\": [\n            \"higher\",\n            \"lower\",\n            \"n/a\"\n           ],\n           \"description\": \"For a company's figure: higher or lower, the end that is good for its business (n/a is refused). For a theme's figure: n/a (higher or lower is refused).\"\n          },\n          \"favourable_why\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"description\": \"For a company's figure: one sentence on why that end is good for the business; it may not be empty. For a theme's figure: empty.\"\n          }\n         }\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      \"reference\": {\n       \"anyOf\": [\n        {\n         \"type\": \"null\"\n        },\n        {\n         \"type\": \"object\",\n         \"additionalProperties\": false,\n         \"required\": [\n          \"words\",\n          \"kind\",\n          \"value\",\n          \"value_to\"\n         ],\n         \"properties\": {\n          \"words\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"pattern\": \"\\\\S\",\n           \"description\": \"The item's own words for what the figure or event is measured against.\"\n          },\n          \"kind\": {\n           \"type\": \"string\",\n           \"enum\": [\n            \"expected\",\n            \"last_reported\"\n           ],\n           \"description\": \"expected: what the market expects. last_reported: for a figure only, the amount most recently reported for the same quantity, when the item states no expectation.\"\n          },\n          \"value\": {\n           \"anyOf\": [\n            {\n             \"type\": \"number\"\n            },\n            {\n             \"type\": \"null\"\n            }\n           ],\n           \"description\": \"The number the item states, in the figure's unit; the lower end of a stated range. Null when the item gives it in words alone.\"\n          },\n          \"value_to\": {\n           \"anyOf\": [\n            {\n             \"type\": \"number\"\n            },\n            {\n             \"type\": \"null\"\n            }\n           ],\n           \"description\": \"The upper end of a stated range; else null.\"\n          }\n         }\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      \"due_day\": {\n       \"type\": \"string\",\n       \"pattern\": \"^\\\\d{4}-\\\\d{2}-\\\\d{2}$\"\n      },\n      \"due_day_basis\": {\n       \"type\": \"string\",\n       \"enum\": [\n        \"stated\",\n        \"period_end\",\n        \"expected_publication\"\n       ],\n       \"description\": \"For a figure: stated or expected_publication. For an event: stated, or period_end (the last day of the period the topic names, or the company's current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision with no date).\"\n      }\n     }\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n }\n}\n# Stage A reading form: is each answer a sound judged question for its thesis, or a justified refusal? (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs. Do not read any other reader's answers.\n\n## What you are reading\n\nThe packet you are given holds a list of items. Each item is one research topic (\"thesis\") a system opened\nwhile reading one news article, with a proposed **judged question** for it:\n- `thesis`: the fields the system wrote when it opened the topic: `description` (the topic's wording), `outcome` (what is tracked),\n  `period`, `opens_as`, `settled_by`;\n- `article`: the opening article's publication date, title and full text;\n- `fiscal_year_note`: how the company names its fiscal years and which is this year and which next year on the article's day,\n  the same note the step was given. Use it wherever a mark turns on a year;\n- `ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it`: the proposed judged question exactly as a later judge will see it: its `object` (the company's\n  ticker NVDA, or a theme id), `kind`, its `focus` (the line the judge is shown beside the question: the thesis's `outcome`),\n  `question`, `year`, `due_day` and its `rungs` (each a label and a condition; exactly one rung\n  is meant to hold on the due day). An event's rungs are `Yes`, then `Ruled out` when the step named something that can rule\n  the event out, then a last rung that code writes as the complement of the others (`Still open`, or `No` when there are\n  two rungs). In the `Ruled out` rung the step wrote the first sentence; the sentence after it is code's (\"This rung\n  holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole taken after <the article's day>, or a formal\n  abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole after that day, stands on <due day>, and only when rung 1 does not\"), so that a\n  rejection the step did not name is still `Ruled out`, and one that was lifted, that concerns only a part of the event, or\n  that was already in place when the article appeared, is not. Or `no_ladder` with a reason: the step\n  declined to write a question;\n- `other_fields`: what the step recorded beside the ladder and the judge does not see: `not_judged` (parts of the topic's wording\n  the question leaves out, with why), `reference` (what the question is measured against, quoted from the article: its `kind` is `expected` for what\n  the article says the market expects, or `last_reported` for the amount most recently reported for a figure when the article\n  states no expectation; its number when the article states one, with `value_to` as the upper end when the article states a\n  range; when it has a number, the judge also sees the quoted passage, inside the middle rung's condition, introduced as \"the\n  reference\" or as \"the amount last reported\"), `favourable_end` and\n  `favourable_why` (for a company's figure: which end is good for the business, and the reason given), `ruled_out_by` (for\n  an event with a `Ruled out` rung: who the step names as the one whose decision would rule the event out; the first sentence\n  of that rung is the decision it names for them), `due_day_basis`.\n\nSome items are sound and some carry a fault. Judge each on its own against its thesis and its article.\n\n## The rules a judged question must meet\n\n- One judged question asks **one** figure for a named period or **one** event: the one the thesis's `outcome` names. Every other\n  part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) must be listed\n  in `not_judged`.\n- An event has at most three outcomes: it has happened by the due day; it is ruled out by the due day though it has not happened\n  (decided against, or formally abandoned or withdrawn); neither. The step writes the first condition and, when something can\n  rule the event out, the way it names for that; the rest is code's, so every outcome has exactly one rung. An event that has\n  happened by the due day is `Yes` even if it is undone afterwards, unless the question asks whether it still stands. What\n  rules an event out is a decision by someone (the company, an authority, a court, a party): the due day arriving without the\n  event, or a decision to go on waiting, is no ruling out, and the step names who decides in `ruled_out_by` (D416). A proceeding\n  an authority conducts, where the question asks for its published decision or conclusion, always has a ruling out: a published\n  closing decision, with or without a penalty, is `Yes`; the authority formally abandoning or withdrawing the proceeding without\n  that decision or conclusion is `Ruled out`; the proceeding still going on the due day is the last rung (D420). A\n  difference inside an outcome (an approval with conditions against one without) is not a rung; when the thesis's wording names\n  it, it belongs in `not_judged`. A figure has five ranges,\n  and exactly one holds for every value: no overlap, no gap.\n- A question about the company's revenue, profit, share, product progress or its own obligations is the company's (object NVDA,\n  kind asset). A question about what happens in the world (licensing, aggregate spending, a policy) is a theme's. A question\n  whose tracked outcome is a government's or regulator's decision to permit, restrict or ban a class of products or trade is the\n  theme's that covers it, also when the product is the company's own; when the tracked outcome is the company's own sales,\n  shipments or revenue under such a decision, or a proceeding against the company itself (an investigation of its conduct, a\n  fine), the question is the company's (D418).\n- A company's figure runs from Strongly bullish to Strongly bearish, meaning good or bad for the company's business. An event's\n  rungs carry no such labels.\n- The reference is what the question is measured against when it opens. Its kind is `expected` for what the market expects: a\n  consensus, the company's guidance, a stated plan or deadline. For a figure, where the article states no expectation and gives\n  the amount most recently reported for the same quantity, that amount is the reference and its kind is `last_reported`; a\n  reported amount is never called an expectation (D411). When the article states a number, the field carries it and a\n  figure's middle rung holds it; a stated range is carried by its two ends, and the middle rung runs from at or below its lower end to at or above its upper end. When the\n  article states it in words alone, the field carries the words and no number, and the ranges are the step's own estimate.\n- The due day is when the question can be resolved: a figure's publication date; for an event, the date its outcome is assessed. Where\n  the thesis or the article states the day a figure is published, that day is right. Where neither does (`due_day_basis` is\n  `expected_publication`), code sets the day and the step's own estimate is not shown: 60 days after the company's fiscal\n  year-end for a company's figure, 90 days after the calendar year-end for a theme's (D414). That day is right when the figure\n  is a figure for that year; it is wrong when the figure the question asks for is not a year's figure (a quarter, a survey's\n  rolling twelve months), which is then a fault of the question's period and of the due day both.\n- A company's figure for a quarter or a half-year is never a judged question: the step must decline it. A ladder written for\n  one is wrong even when it asks exactly what the thesis names (mark `Y`). An annual figure that only names quarterly reports as\n  the material that settles it is not such a case.\n- A step may decline (`no_ladder`) only in the seven cases its instructions allow: the thesis has no main figure or event (its\n  outcome names none, or several things and none is the main one); nothing in it can be checked on a date (for an event:\n  neither the thesis nor the article gives a date, and the thesis names no period, unless it is a pending regulatory decision,\n  which is due at the company's fiscal year-end); it is the company's figure for a\n  quarter or a half-year; it is a figure for a period that is not one fiscal or calendar year, such as a survey's rolling twelve\n  months, and neither the thesis nor the article gives the day it is published (D417); it is a figure for a year that had already\n  ended on the article's day; it is a figure for a year beyond\n  next year, unless the thesis's `opens_as` is binding_obligation; or the question belongs to another company, or to a matter that\n  none of the listed themes covers (the listed themes are given with the packet). Declining for any other reason, or when the case\n  named does not truly apply, is not justified.\n\n## What to return\n\nOne JSON object per item, one per line, in item order, as your final message: no prose before or after, no code fence. Fields:\n\n| field | values | the mark |\n|---|---|---|\n| `item` | the item number | |\n| `Q` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the judged question is worded as a question, not as an assertion, and asks the thing the thesis's `outcome` names. For a figure it also names the period the figure covers, or the day it is measured at, and the material that settles it. And it does not ask something other than its `focus` line (a focus that also names parts listed in `not_judged` is expected and is not a conflict). It can be understood by a reader who never sees the article: it never says \"this item\" or leans on a figure or a name it does not give. A group named as the trade names it (\"the big four AI hyperscalers\", \"the top five hyperscalers\") is a name the question gives: a question is not failed for not listing the group's members. `n/a` for a `no_ladder` |\n| `O` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the object is the question's own company or the right theme, by the rule above: a tracked government decision on a class of products is the theme's; the company's own sales under it, and a proceeding against the company, are the company's |\n| `R` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | exactly one rung holds for every outcome. For a figure: the ranges meet end to end. For an event, where code writes the last rung: the `Yes` condition is the event the question asks about; the first sentence of the `Ruled out` condition, when there is one, is a decision against that event as a whole, or its abandonment or withdrawal as a whole, taken after the article's day and standing on the due day, and not the event's merely being still awaited, the refusal of one part of it, or a bar already in place when the article appeared (it need not list every way: code's sentence takes the others); and where the event can plainly be ruled out before the due day (a formal refusal, a block, an announced abandonment; for an authority's proceeding, its formal abandonment or withdrawal without the asked-for decision or conclusion) and the answer has no `Ruled out` rung, the mark fails; it fails too when `ruled_out_by` names no one who decides, or when the decision named is the day arriving, the event not having happened, or a decision to go on with a proceeding. Every condition is concrete and can be checked on the due day: none rests on a target or a threshold that is not yet set |\n| `D` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the due day follows the rule for its kind. A figure: the day the thesis or the article states, or, where neither states one, the day code sets from the year the figure covers (60 days after the company's fiscal year-end; 90 days after the calendar year-end for a theme's figure), which holds when the figure asked for is that year's figure. An event: the date the thesis or the article gives for assessing it, or the last day of the period it names, or the company's fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision with no date. It fails when the day contradicts a date the thesis or the article gives, falls before the figure's period ends, or, for an event, is a date none of these gives And `due_day_basis` says truly where the day comes from: `stated` only when the thesis or the article gives that day; `period_end` only when it is the last day of the period the thesis names, or the fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision; `expected_publication` when the step set the day it expects the figure to be published. For a thesis whose `opens_as` is binding_obligation: an obligation already taken on is due at its next annual filing, and one not yet signed on the day by which the signing can be checked; another day fails |\n| `X` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | a figure: a number the `reference` carries lies in the middle rung, as the judge will read it (for a stated range: the middle rung runs from at or below its lower end to at or above its upper end); a reference with no number, or none, has nothing to place and holds, unless the ladder presents as expected something the article does not state. An event: the ladder presents as expected only what the article states |\n| `F` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a company's figure only: the direction is defensible (which end is bullish), and the reason given in `favourable_why` supports the favourable end. For an amount the company owes, guarantees or has committed to pay, where neither the thesis nor the article settles which end is good for the business (they argue both ends or say nothing on it), the lower end is the favourable one (D413), and \"higher\" fails unless the thesis's own words say that more of it is good. `n/a` for an event and for a theme |\n| `Y` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a figure only: the year, the unit and the scale of the numbers are the question's (an amount in full; a percentage as 31, not 0.31), and the figure is a whole year's. A company's figure for a quarter or a half-year fails, whatever the thesis asks |\n| `G` | `holds`, `fails`, `not_assessable`, `n/a` | for a figure, always one of the first three: where the article gives any figure for this quantity, `holds` when an analyst would accept the ranges as a fair division of the outcomes around it and `fails` when not; `not_assessable` when the article gives none. `n/a` only for an event's ladder and for a `no_ladder` |\n| `L` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | every other part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) is listed in `not_judged`, each with a reason that says why it is left out; a wording that asks nothing else holds with an empty list |\n| `B` | a list | exactly one entry for each part listed in `not_judged`, in its order: `\"bet\"` when an analyst would hold a separate view on it, `\"not_a_bet\"` when it is a detail of the judged question; `[]` when nothing is listed and for a `no_ladder` |\n| `XR` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the `reference` field quotes what the article states the market expects, with `kind` `expected`, or, for a figure where the article states no expectation, the amount most recently reported for the same quantity (the same thing measured the same way: not a part of it, a ceiling on it or another quantity), with `kind` `last_reported`; the kind says truly which. It carries the number when the article states one (a range by its two ends), and no number the article does not state. It is null only when the article states no expectation and, for a figure, no amount last reported for the same quantity either; an event's is null whenever the article states no expectation |\n| `N` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a `no_ladder` only: the refusal is justified by one of the allowed cases, and that case truly applies. A refusal because the question belongs to another company or to a matter no listed theme covers names that company or matter. Which year is this year and which next year is read from the `fiscal_year_note` |\n| `W` | a string | any other fault that would mislead a reader of this judged question, in a short sentence; the empty string `\"\"` when there is none. The field is always present |\n| `why` | a string | one sentence giving the reason for every `fails` and for a non-empty `W`, naming the words or the figure; it may be empty only when no mark fails and `W` is empty |\n\nRead each item's article before answering it. Judge only what is in the item.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
