{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'not_judged' clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder\"","aggregated_output":"clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:25:- `other_fields`: what the step recorded beside the ladder and the judge does not see: `not_judged` (parts of the topic's wording\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:41:  in `not_judged`.\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:52:  it, it belongs in `not_judged`. A figure has five ranges,\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:95:| `Q` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the judged question is worded as a question, not as an assertion, and asks the thing the thesis's `outcome` names. For a figure it also names the period the figure covers, or the day it is measured at, and the material that settles it. And it does not ask something other than its `focus` line (a focus that also names parts listed in `not_judged` is expected and is not a conflict). It can be understood by a reader who never sees the article: it never says \"this item\" or leans on a figure or a name it does not give. A group named as the trade names it (\"the big four AI hyperscalers\", \"the top five hyperscalers\") is a name the question gives: a question is not failed for not listing the group's members. `n/a` for a `no_ladder` |\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:103:| `L` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | every other part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) is listed in `not_judged`, each with a reason that says why it is left out; a wording that asks nothing else holds with an empty list |\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md:104:| `B` | a list | exactly one entry for each part listed in `not_judged`, in its order: `\"bet\"` when an analyst would hold a separate view on it, `\"not_a_bet\"` when it is a detail of the judged question; `[]` when nothing is listed and for a `no_ladder` |\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:5:- A topic's wording may ask about more than one thing. Judge the one the topic's outcome names as what is tracked. List every other part of the topic's wording that the question leaves out, a second figure or event, a condition or a comparison, in not_judged, each with one sentence on why it is not the one tracked. Never fold two things into one ladder: \"Will the regulator approve the drug for a second use, and how many patients will take it in the first year?\" is judged on the approval when that is what its outcome names, and the patient count is listed; \"How much will the carrier's fuel bill be next year, and will its pilots strike?\" is judged on the fuel bill when that is what its outcome names, and the strike is listed.\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-system.txt:13:- An event is answered in event, in at most three outcomes. happened is the condition under which the event the question asks about has happened by the due day. ruled_out says who can rule the event as a whole out before the due day, and how: by_whom names the company, authority, court or party whose decision it would be; decision is one full sentence that names them and says what they decide against the event as a whole, or that they formally abandon or withdraw it, after the item's day. The due day arriving without the event is no one's decision and is never written there: it is what is left. ruled_out is null only when no one's decision can rule the event out before the due day. Write no other condition: what is left, neither of the two, needs none, and where both would hold the event counts as happened. A difference inside an outcome, such as an approval with conditions against one without, is not an outcome of its own; when the topic's wording names it, list it in not_judged. A regulator reviewing a merger gives: happened, the regulator has cleared the merger by the due day; ruled_out, by_whom the regulator or the parties to the merger, decision the regulator blocks the merger or the parties withdraw the filing. An authority's investigation or examination that the question asks to end in a published decision or conclusion always has a ruling out: happened, the authority publishes that decision or conclusion by the due day, whatever it finds; ruled_out, by_whom the authority, decision the authority formally abandons or withdraws the proceeding without it. The proceeding still going on the due day is neither, and a decision to go on with it is not a ruling out. A safety agency examining a carmaker's defect gives: happened, the agency publishes its findings; ruled_out, by_whom the agency, decision the agency formally closes or withdraws the examination without publishing any.\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-closing.txt:1:Before you return the answer, check it once. Read the question back: does it ask one thing, the one the topic's outcome names, and is every other part of the topic's wording listed in not_judged? For a figure, check that each range starts exactly where the one below it ends. For an event, check that happened is the event the question asks about; that ruled_out names who decides and a decision of theirs against that event as a whole or its abandonment, never the due day arriving or the event merely being still awaited, and is null only when no one's decision can rule the event out; and that each can be checked on the due day. Check that a reference's kind says truly whether its words state an expectation or the amount already reported for the same quantity, that its value is the number its words state, and, for a figure, that the value falls in the middle range, or that the middle range runs from at or below a stated range's lower end to at or above its upper end, that every bound is a figure and not a future target, and that the due day follows the rules above. For an amount the company owes, guarantees or has committed to pay, check that favourable_end is lower unless the topic or the item settles the other end or the topic's own words say that more of it is good. Correct the answer where a check fails.\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:17:    \"not_judged\": [],\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:63:    \"not_judged\": [\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:118:    \"not_judged\": [\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:176:    \"not_judged\": [\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:238:    \"not_judged\": [],\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:332:    \"not_judged\": [],\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/examples.json:454:    \"not_judged\": [],\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-schema.json:32:      \"not_judged\",\nclean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/candidate-schema.json:50:      \"not_judged\": {\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/second-week/proofs/audit-cases.json\\\")); a=p[\\\"event_reference_cases_failed_or_open\\\"]; [(print(x[\\\"reading\\\"],x[\\\"case\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE:\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"sentence\\\"],\\\"ATTEMPTS:\\\",[(z[\\\"attempt\\\"],z[\\\"reference_words\\\"],z[\\\"mark\\\"]) for z in x[\\\"attempts\\\"]])) for x in a]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/second-week/proofs/audit-cases.json\\\")); a=p[\\\"event_reference_cases_failed_or_open\\\"]; [(print(x[\\\"reading\\\"],x[\\\"case\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE:\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"][\\\"sentence\\\"],\\\"ATTEMPTS:\\\",[(z[\\\"attempt\\\"],z[\\\"reference_words\\\"],z[\\\"mark\\\"]) for z in x[\\\"attempts\\\"]])) for x in a]' \"","aggregated_output":"Alex's 44 topics u1/clean-11 ARTICLE: China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\nChinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\nThe companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\nThe chips have emerged as a geopolitical flas ATTEMPTS: [(1, 'China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia', 'holds'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, 'China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia', 'holds')]\nAlex's 44 topics u1/clean-6 ARTICLE: The Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\nKyber allows GPUs to be mounted in compute trays that sit vertically rather than horizontally, a design that Nvidia has described as being “like books on a shelf,” and which reportedly boosts density and reduces latency.\nHowever, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback.\n“Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has bee ATTEMPTS: [(1, 'had originally been slated for release in 2027', 'holds'), (2, 'had originally been slated for release in 2027', 'open'), (3, 'pushing its release date to 2028', 'holds')]\nAlex's 44 topics u3/clean-3 ARTICLE: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. ATTEMPTS: [(1, 'could begin shipping later this year', 'holds'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, 'could begin shipping later this year', 'holds')]\nAlex's 44 topics u3/clean-9 ARTICLE: Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'holds'), (2, None, 'open'), (3, None, 'holds')]\nJuly c1/clean-14 ARTICLE: Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'fails'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, None, 'fails')]\nJuly c1/clean-3 ARTICLE: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'fails'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, 'could begin shipping later this year', 'holds')]\nJuly c2/clean-2 ARTICLE: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'fails'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, None, 'fails')]\nJuly c2/clean-5 ARTICLE: The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\n\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board tha ATTEMPTS: [(1, \"and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\", 'holds'), (2, \"had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027\", 'open'), (3, \"had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027\", 'holds')]\nJuly c2/clean-9 ARTICLE: Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'fails'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, None, 'fails')]\nJuly c3/clean-12 ARTICLE: Regulators are simultaneously intensifying their oversight, with the French competition authority announcing Thursday that its antitrust investigation into the company is nearing a conclusion. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'fails'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, None, 'fails')]\nthe week of 10-06 c1/clean-7 ARTICLE: Its newest chip, Rubin, should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter, and industry reports say the ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. ATTEMPTS: [(1, None, 'open'), (2, 'should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter', 'open'), (3, 'should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter', 'open')]\nthe week of 10-06 c1/clean-9 ARTICLE: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) said Groq 3 LPX, its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology, is already in full production, with volume shipments expected later this quarter. ATTEMPTS: [(1, 'volume shipments expected later this quarter', 'holds'), (2, None, 'fails'), (3, 'volume shipments expected later this quarter', 'holds')]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"The request leaves two boundaries unclear: what counts as an event expectation, and which topic fields count as its wording. The packet also shows inconsistent reader marks, so not every failed mark is evidence of a prompt failure.\n\n### A. Event references\n\nThe request says: “reference is what this figure or event is measured against,” and its kind is `expected` when the item states “a consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline.” For figures only, a recent reported amount can be used; “Otherwise reference is null.” The schema says `expected` is “what the market expects.”\n\nThe form’s `XR` says the reference quotes “what the article states the market expects” and an event’s is null whenever the article states no expectation. `X` says an event presents as expected only what the article states. They use similar words, but neither defines whether a progress report, an older plan, or an expectation about a related measure counts as the event’s expectation.\n\n| Article expectation | Careful reading of request; attempts |\n|---|---|\n| China **plans to allow** purchases | Quote required; 2 of 3 quoted. |\n| Rubin **could begin shipping later this year** | Quote required for first shipments; across three topics, quoted in 2/3, 1/3 and 0/3. |\n| Groq **volume shipments expected later this quarter** | Quote required; 2 of 3 quoted. |\n| Kyber **slated for release/debut in 2027**, versus reported delay to 2028 | The stated plan is to be quoted, but whether release or debut is the same as first shipments is open. All attempts quoted; one used the 2028 delay. |\n| Investigation **nearing a conclusion** | Open: progress suggests timing, but is not plainly a stated plan or deadline. All 12 attempts were null. |\n| Rubin **20% of revenue**, ramp **could slip** | The 20% is a different measure; the ramp forecast’s status as the reference is open. One attempt was null and two quoted 20%. |\n\n**Marks were not consistent.** The same investigation sentence and null reference held in two attempts in Alex’s reading but failed across July topics. The same Kyber debut quotation held and was left open. Questions differ, so the packet does not prove every difference is a reader error.\n\nThe judge does **not** see an event’s reference: `write_view` builds event conditions without it and returns no reference passage.\n\nVerdicts: **misread** for the clear plans or shipment expectations omitted; **abstract** for the boundary cases, because the request gives categories but no example resolving them. **Not the prompt** also applies to inconsistent marks and the judge’s missing reference.\n\n### B. Unlisted parts\n\nCounts below are attempts listing each part, out of three; “declined” means no list was produced. Placement is based on the topic text; whether a detail is independently left out can depend on the individual question.\n\n| Topic | Description beyond the question | Outcome-only parts |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | — | Paid/accrued; remaining term: **1/3 each** |\n| 2 | Unconditional status: **3/3**, as adjustable/cancellable | — |\n| 3 | — | Average prices; margins: **2/3 each** |\n| 4 | Both years: **all declined** | Forecast comparisons: **all declined** |\n| 5 | Final cost; regulatory conditions: **3/3 each** | — |\n| 6 | — | China revenue resumption: **3/3** |\n| 7 | Shortage cause/slip: **3/3** | Revenue share; sales timing: **3/3 each** |\n| 8 | 25% loss coverage: **1/3** | Paid/accrued; terms/duration: **3/3 each** |\n| 9 | Commercial scale: **3/3** | Revenue contribution; later slip: **3/3 each** |\n| 10 | Earlier price comparison: **2/2 answered** | — |\n| 11 | Signing: **1/3**; consideration/terms: **3/3** | Regulatory conditions: explicit **2/3** |\n| 12 | Both years: **all declined** | Forecast comparisons: **all declined** |\n| 13 | Funding versus outstanding: funding **1/1 answered** | Changed commitments: **1/1 answered** |\n\nThe request says “List every other part of the topic’s wording that the question leaves out,” and the closing check repeats it. It never identifies which fields constitute that wording.\n\nD547’s **undefined-field finding holds**. Its earlier count cannot be generalized from this packet: outcome-only parts range from **1/3 to 3/3**, and the new two agreed failures concern different topics. Verdict: **missing**, because the field boundary is unspecified. The inspected judge view omits the list; D397 requires it shown beside the answer, but this code alone does not establish whether a separate product view does so.\n\n### C. Whether to design repairs\n\nA reference repair is worth considering for consistent recording, but the judge gains nothing until it can see the reference. It could wrongly equate a revenue share with a shipment ramp or erase a valid plan. Preserve Groq’s quoted expectation and valid null cases; the packet does not supply their articles to independently recheck every null.\n\nAn unlisted-parts repair is worth designing because readers should see what the answer leaves out. It could clutter the answer with context or split one bet into fragments. Preserve the correctly listed acquisition cost and conditions, and the outcome-only parts already listed for topics 1 and 3."}}
